bola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-1,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+1=2pt,\\,y-2=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(1,4),$ we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=4$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2 &=2pt \\\\ 2 &=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+1=2(\\frac{1}{2})t,\\,y-2=(\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+1=t,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186241180 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241180 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106455" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241180 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241180 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(3,3)$ and which passes through the point $(7,5)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241180 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "36c7a66b-6181-5e37-8eb4-d8f285049a59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241180 #edb/ref 1074 "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(3,3),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-3=2pt,\\,y-3=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(7,5),$ we can substitute $x=7$ and $y=5$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4 &=2pt \\\\ 2 &=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{2}{p} \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-3=2(2)t,\\,y-3=(2){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-3=4t,\\,y-3=2{t}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186241183 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241183 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106461" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241183 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241183 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e5/e5c7f025b6f12599a4ecb5fa0cc9e657fd0b67deb1e50231673113f337ca7776.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241183 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "53bdcf57-c309-5f31-a918-fde0b0925ad9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241183 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(4,4),$ and passes through the point $(8,8).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(4,4),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-4=p{t}^{2},\\,y-4=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(8,8),$ we can substitute $x=8$ and $y=8$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4 &=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 4 &=2pt.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 4 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{4}{4} \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-4=(1){t}^{2},\\,y-4=2(1)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-4={t}^{2},\\,y-4=2t.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186241214 #edb/ref 1080 "(y-5)^{2}=-8(x-2)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241214 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241214 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "03e4ca77-f2af-5110-bdff-f4762cbcd8da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241236 #edb/ref 1080 "(x-5)^{2}=-8(y-2)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241236 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241236 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "064d8cce-843b-5ad0-87ca-f878c2e21278" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241503 #edb/ref 1080 "(x-2)^{2}=8(y-5)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241503 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241503 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "25bad7a1-76dd-58ae-9f5b-e2231867653d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241743 #edb/ref 1080 "(x-2)^{2}=-8(y-5)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241743 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241743 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3ef6ba9e-c87d-52c1-9732-615a663d486a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242412 #edb/ref 1080 "(y-5)^{2}=8(x-2)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242412 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242412 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8b335e25-8957-510c-896e-d1259089977e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242412 #ed