1075 #edb/ref 17592186353374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354242 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354242 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354242 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186351811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354242 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f59570fd-6d43-585f-9b41-1fb122f41d08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354344 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354344 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84819" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354344 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354344 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d9/d963d5620df91a05c8371ef09beeb84546c5f5a8cda047f2c2c3b9933f3d5c01.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354344 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7c9ce835-d453-5565-8c95-6082447bc008" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354344 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(2,-2),$ and passes through the point $(4,2).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(2,-2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-2=2pt,\\,y+2=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,2),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=2$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2 &=2pt \\\\ 4 &=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 4 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-2=2(\\frac{1}{4})t,\\,y+2=(\\frac{1}{4}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-2=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186354349 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354349 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84840" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354349 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354349 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/0d/0d278fe1bf34cd60877be6886c90e1734fab7433f9f94ad7765d379e69c2da42.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354349 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d45f6ac-8de5-558d-a5e4-8428086d3905" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354349 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(2,2),$ and passes through the point $(4,6).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(2,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-2=p{t}^{2},\\,y-2=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,6),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=6$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2 &=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 4 &=2pt.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-2=(2){t}^{2},\\,y-2=2(2)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-2=2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=4t.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186354353 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354353 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84844" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354353 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354353 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y-3)^{2}=-12(x-5)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354353 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1303c715-2341-5c20-852f-40641cdc1a09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354353 #edb/ref 1074 "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(5,3).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx-5=p{t}^{2},\\,y-3=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =-12(x-5) \\\\ 4p(x-5) & =-12(x-5) \\\\ 4p & =-12 \\\\ p & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-5=(-3){t}^{2},\\,y-3=2(-3)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-5=-3{t}^{2},\\,y-3=-6t.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354354 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354354 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84847" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354354 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354354 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y-2)^{2}=16(x+6)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354354 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f197f0f5-2e24-5c55-8528-25906de0f7eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354354 #edb/ref 1074 "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-6,2).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx+6=p{t}^{2},\\,y-2=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =16(x+6) \\\\ 4p(x+6) & =16(x+6) \\\\ 4p & =16 \\\\ p & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+6=(4){t}^{2},\\,y-2=2(4)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+6=4{t}^{2},\\,y-2=8t.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354360 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354360 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84859" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354360 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354360 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+1=-\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-\\frac{5}{2}t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354360 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5f7f6549-2721-5283-9cbb-bd6bbc1d1262" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354360 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-\\frac{4(x+1)}{5},\\,t=-\\frac{2(y-1)}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{2(y-1)}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(y-1)^{2}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{4(x+1)}{5}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4(y-1)^{2}}{25} & =-\\frac{4(x+1)}{5} \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}\\cdot (-\\frac{4(x+1)}{5}) \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =-5(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428775 #edb/ref 1030 0.5 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428775 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 2837->1661. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428775 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186048647 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428776 #edb/ref 1030 0.25 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428776 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 4 eligible passed source activities = 0.250000; nearest selected fraction = 1/4. Crossing activity numbers [3]; waits [3]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1/4 (1/4). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 2837->2978. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186